From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Artur Szafraniec <ganggg111@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 23:05:31 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Input: s6sy761 - fix the count of the events still queued

Byte 7 of an event carries that count in its low five bits only.  Bit 5 is
the max energy flag of the touch point and the two bits above it are
the low half of the touch type, so a six bit mask adds 32 to the count
whenever the controller raises that flag.

The driver then finds the count larger than the event buffer and returns
without handling anything, dropping the event it had already read together
with the rest of the batch.  Release events go missing that way, and a slot
that never hears about the finger lifting stays busy for good: read back
from a Galaxy S10+ with nobody touching the screen, three slots still held
tracking ids 12, 7 and 10.  Userspace sees three fingers pressed forever.

The layout is the one the vendor driver of this panel describes, where the
field was narrowed from six bits to five and the freed bit became the flag.
A count above 31 could not be used anyway, since the buffer holds 32
events.

Signed-off-by: Artur Szafraniec <ganggg111@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/input/touchscreen/s6sy761.c | 7 ++++++-
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/input/touchscreen/s6sy761.c b/drivers/input/touchscreen/s6sy761.c
index 4d78c7cb3ab..fcbb5d3f380 100644
--- a/drivers/input/touchscreen/s6sy761.c
+++ b/drivers/input/touchscreen/s6sy761.c
@@ -69,7 +69,12 @@
 #define S6SY761_MASK_X			0xf0 /* byte 3 */
 #define S6SY761_MASK_Y			0x0f
 #define S6SY761_MASK_Z			0x3f /* byte 6 */
-#define S6SY761_MASK_LEFT_EVENTS	0x3f /* byte 7 */
+/*
+ * Byte 7 carries the number of events still queued in its low five bits.
+ * Bit 5 is the max energy flag of the touch point, and bits 6 and 7 are
+ * the low half of the touch type, so none of them belongs to the count.
+ */
+#define S6SY761_MASK_LEFT_EVENTS	0x1f /* byte 7 */
 #define S6SY761_MASK_TOUCH_TYPE		0xc0 /* MSB in byte 6, LSB in byte 7 */
 
 /* event touch state values */
